#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ddr -- the project's command surface: the oracle, the build, both gates, the diff.

Every routine step of the matching loop is one verb here, so a contributor (or an agent)
never has to remember which script, which config path or which order.  The verbs are thin
wrappers by design: ``configure.py`` owns the build graph, ``tools/verify/g1.py`` the
whole-file gate, ``tools/verify/g2.py`` the localisation, ``tools/iso/reinsert.py`` the disc
image, ``tools/oracle/*`` the artifacts, and ``tools/toolchain/check_deps.py`` the host
tools.  Nothing is re-implemented in this file; the one decision it makes is *which* of
those to call, in which order, and what to print.

Which *target* they work on is decided in exactly one place, ``config/targets.py``: the retail
ELF and its G1 pin, the disc image and its G3 pin, the config directory, the measured geometry
and the per-unit build pairs all come from the descriptor named by ``--target`` (default:
the target the tree is configured for).  ``--target`` is accepted before or after the verb.

    ./ddr oracle            regenerate the symbol/CU/chunk/header artifacts from the target's
                            retail ELF (deterministic: a fresh run reproduces what is
                            committed), applying its borrowed_symbols.txt -- the library
                            names tools/sdk/borrow.py validated against the corpora -- to
                            the symbol ledger
    ./ddr build             configure (manifests -> splat inputs -> build.ninja) then ninja
    ./ddr verify            G1: the target's orig/<elf> must be the pinned retail binary, and
                            its out/<elf> must equal it
    ./ddr verify --tu NAME  ... plus the per-TU verdict: its .text span, its .debug chunk
                            and its .line chunks against retail (the promotion rule)
    ./ddr verify --iso IMG  ... plus G3: the built ELF spliced back into a copy of IMG must
                            give IMG back, hash for hash
    ./ddr diff SYM TU       objdiff-cli between the target's *retail bytes* for SYM and the
                            object under edit: the C object the graph compiles TU's source
                            to, or -- a unit no C source exists for -- our own bytes for SYM
                            out of its split.  The target side is built from the retail image
                            (``tools/diff/basis.py``) rather than from the split, whose
                            unresolved call targets depress the score of a legitimately
                            identical function; both sides are printed with what they are, so a
                            retail-bytes diff is never taken for a split one
    ./ddr iso --iso IMG     G3 on its own

On a G1 failure the offset is localised through the oracle's manifests -- ``.debug``/``.line``
by G1's own chunk walk, a code byte by the DWARF function compile unit that covers it -- and
printed as one line naming the translation unit, the function and the compile unit (R32:
G2 consumes G1's localisation instead of deriving a second one).

Exit codes: 0 the verb's work succeeded and every gate it ran was green; 1 a gate or a TU
verdict failed, or a host tool is missing (``check_deps`` prints the remedy, never a
traceback); 2 a usage or configuration problem -- an unknown target, a target whose geometry
nobody has measured yet, a missing input, a name that matches no translation unit, an image
the ELF does not fit into, an image no record in the target's ``checksums.txt`` names
(``--allow-unpinned-reference`` is the opt-out), a symbol objdiff-cli compared nothing for
(there is no score to print for it).
"""

